    "2b. What asymptote does the plot seem to approach? Why is this?\n",
    "\n",
    "Answer: The plot seems to approach an asymptotic minimum read number to coverage ratio of 0.5. This is because as coverage increases, the number of observations also increases, so it's easier to view deviations from the expected 50% incidence rate. From a mathematical perspective, the minimum number of minor allele reads is dependent on the expected number, 0.5n, and standard deviation, which scales with sqrt(n) where n is the coverage. Thus as coverage increases, so does the difference between the minimum number and the expected number. However, when the minimum number is divided by the coverage, n, we get sqrt(n) in the denominator instead, which pushes the term to 0. This causes 0.5n to be the surviving term, which is why the minor allele count to coverage ratio approaches 0.5 asymptotically."
