   "id": "773338ba",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Answer:\n",
    "Random guessing would achieve an accuracy of 0.2 since there are 5 populations and 100 samples in the validation set, so we have a 1/5 chance for each randomly guessing it correct. This corresponds with an accuracy of 0.2. Based on the proximity of these two clusters in the T-SNE and UMAP plots, and the PCA data, I would expect the Pluto and Saturn populations to be confused. Also based on the PCA data, maybe the Venus and Mars populations can be confused too. However, since we're using K nearest neighbors, the proximity problem would likely be the more relevant one. The confusion matrix is inconclusive regarding that expectation since it performed perfectly across the validation data, so whether or not the model was most likely to confuse these two populations cannot be determined. It appears that it did a good job at classifying all populations, which could correlate to the high degree of separation between all populations in the T-SNE and UMAP plots. Not necessarily; overlap in a 2D plot could simply suggest the distances between two populations isn't great, but there could be other features in the populations that can contribute to a good classifier. "
